1
00:00:01,799 --> 00:00:05,159
>> No animal shall drink alcohol.

2
00:00:05,159 --> 00:00:08,840
>> But there were two words that they had forgotten.

3
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:16,399
Actually, the commandment read >> No animal shall drink alcohol to excess.

4
00:00:16,719 --> 00:00:23,520
>> Chapter 9 Boxer's split hoof was a long time in healing.

5
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:27,040
They had started the rebuilding of the windmill the day after the victory

6
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,399
celebrations were ended.

7
00:00:29,399 --> 00:00:35,120
Boxer refused to take even a day off work and made it a point of honor not to

8
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:38,319
let it be seen that he was in pain.

9
00:00:38,319 --> 00:00:45,079
In the evenings, he would admit privately to Clover that the hoof troubled him a great deal.

10
00:00:45,079 --> 00:00:49,760
Clover treated the hoof with poultices of herbs, which she prepared by chewing

11
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:55,760
them, and both she and Benjamin urged Boxer to work less hard.

12
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:59,159
>> A horse's lungs do not last forever.

13
00:00:59,159 --> 00:01:00,719
>> She said to him.

14
00:01:00,719 --> 00:01:02,680
But Boxer would not listen.

15
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:10,399
He had, he said, only one real ambition left, to see the windmill well underway before

16
00:01:10,399 --> 00:01:13,440
he reached the age for retirement.

17
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:18,360
At the beginning, when the laws of Animal Farm were first formulated,

18
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:25,680
the retiring age had been fixed for horses and pigs at 12, for cows at 14,

19
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:34,280
for dogs at nine, for sheep at seven, and for hens and geese at five.

20
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:38,120
Liberal old age pensions had been agreed upon.

21
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:44,079
As yet, no animal had actually retired on pension, but of late, the subject had been

22
00:01:44,079 --> 00:01:47,040
discussed more and more.

23
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:51,879
Now that the small field beyond the orchard had been set aside for barley,

24
00:01:51,879 --> 00:01:56,479
it was rumored that a corner of the large pasture was to be fenced off and

25
00:01:56,479 --> 00:02:01,760
turned into a grazing ground for superannuated animals.

26
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:07,879
For a horse, it was said, the pension would be 5 lb of corn a day, and in

27
00:02:07,879 --> 00:02:16,799
winter, 15 lb of hay with a carrot or possibly an apple on public holidays.

28
00:02:16,799 --> 00:02:21,920
Boxer's 12th birthday was due in the late summer of the following year.

29
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:24,680
Meanwhile, life was hard.

30
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:30,719
The winter was as cold as the last one had been, and food was even shorter.

31
00:02:30,719 --> 00:02:37,200
Once again, all rations were reduced, except those of the pigs and the dogs.

32
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:42,840
A too rigid equality in rations, Squealer explained, would have been contrary to the

33
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:45,360
principles of animalism.

34
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:49,639
In any case, he had no difficulty in proving to the other animals that they

35
00:02:49,639 --> 00:02:55,680
were not, in reality, short of food, whatever the appearances might be.

36
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:01,799
For the time being, certainly, it had been found necessary to make a readjustment of rations.

37
00:03:01,799 --> 00:03:07,399
Squealer always spoke of it as a readjustment, never as a reduction.

38
00:03:07,399 --> 00:03:12,840
But in comparison with the days of Jones, the improvement was enormous.

39
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:18,680
Reading out the figures in a shrill, rapid voice, he proved to them in detail that they

40
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:24,760
had more oats, more hay, more turnips than they had had in Jones's day,

41
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:29,760
that they worked shorter hours, that their drinking water was of better quality,

42
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:35,479
that they lived longer, that a larger proportion of their young ones survived infancy,

43
00:03:35,479 --> 00:03:40,559
and that they had more straw in their stalls and suffered less from fleas.

44
00:03:40,559 --> 00:03:43,479
The animals believed every word of it.

45
00:03:43,479 --> 00:03:49,959
Truth to tell, Jones and all he stood for had almost faded out of their memories.

46
00:03:49,959 --> 00:03:57,079
They knew that life nowadays was harsh and bare, that they were often hungry and often cold,

47
00:03:57,079 --> 00:04:00,440
and that they were usually working when they were not asleep.

48
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:03,719
But doubtless it had been worse in the old days.

49
00:04:03,719 --> 00:04:05,879
They were glad to believe so.

50
00:04:05,879 --> 00:04:11,239
Besides, in those days they had been slaves, and now they were free,

51
00:04:11,239 --> 00:04:16,200
and that made all the difference, as Squealer did not fail to point out.

52
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:19,079
There were many more mouths to feed now.

53
00:04:19,079 --> 00:04:23,639
In the autumn, the four sows had all littered about simultaneously, producing

54
00:04:23,639 --> 00:04:26,840
31 young pigs between them.

55
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:32,239
The young pigs were piebald, and as Napoleon was the only boar on the farm,

56
00:04:32,239 --> 00:04:35,719
it was possible to guess at their parentage.

57
00:04:35,719 --> 00:04:40,000
It was announced that later, when bricks and timber had been purchased, a

58
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,559
schoolroom would be built in the farmhouse garden.

59
00:04:43,559 --> 00:04:47,680
For the time being, the young pigs were given their instruction by Napoleon

60
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:50,719
himself in the farmhouse kitchen.

61
00:04:50,719 --> 00:04:54,760
They took their exercise in the garden and were discouraged from playing with

62
00:04:54,760 --> 00:04:57,159
the other young animals.

63
00:04:57,159 --> 00:05:02,680
About this time, too, it was laid down as a rule that when a pig and any other

64
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:07,319
animal met on the path, the other animal must stand aside.

65
00:05:07,319 --> 00:05:12,280
And also that all pigs, of whatever degree, were to have the privilege of

66
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:16,000
wearing green ribbons on their tails on Sundays.

67
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,520
The farm had had a fairly successful year, but was still short of money.

68
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:24,920
There were the bricks, sand, and lime for the schoolroom to be purchased, and

69
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:29,959
it would also be necessary to begin saving up again for the machinery for the windmill.

70
00:05:29,959 --> 00:05:35,680
Then there were lamp oil and candles for the house, sugar for Napoleon's own table, he

71
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:39,879
forbade this to the other pigs on the ground that it made them fat, and all

72
00:05:39,879 --> 00:05:49,360
the usual replacements, such as tools, nails, string, coal, wire, scrap iron, and dog biscuits.

73
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:54,000
A stump of hay and part of the potato crop was sold off, and the contract for

74
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,920
eggs was increased to 600 a week, so that that year the hens barely hatched

75
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:02,159
enough chicks to keep their numbers at the same level.

76
00:06:02,159 --> 00:06:07,200
Rations, reduced in December, were reduced again in February, and lanterns

77
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:10,239
in the stalls were forbidden to save oil.

78
00:06:10,239 --> 00:06:16,559
But the pigs seemed comfortable enough, and in fact were putting on weight if anything.

79
00:06:16,559 --> 00:06:21,840
One afternoon in late February, a warm, rich, appetizing scent, such as the

80
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:26,120
animals had never smelt before, wafted itself across the yard from the little

81
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:32,040
brew house, which had been disused in Jones's time, and which stood beyond the kitchen.

82
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:35,360
Someone said it was the smell of cooking barley.

83
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:39,680
The animals sniffed the air hungrily and wondered whether a warm mash was being

84
00:06:39,680 --> 00:06:41,760
prepared for their supper.

85
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:46,159
But no warm mash appeared, and then on the following Sunday, it was announced

86
00:06:46,159 --> 00:06:50,799
that from now onwards all barley would be reserved for the pigs.

87
00:06:50,799 --> 00:06:54,639
The field beyond the orchard had already been sown with barley,

88
00:06:54,639 --> 00:06:59,239
and the news soon leaked out that every pig was now receiving a ration of a pint

89
00:06:59,239 --> 00:07:03,840
of beer daily, with half a gallon for Napoleon himself, which was always

90
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:07,040
served to him in the Crown Derby soup tureen.

91
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:11,399
But if there were hardships to be borne, they were partly offset by the fact that

92
00:07:11,399 --> 00:07:16,680
life nowadays had a greater dignity than it had had before.

93
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:22,200
There were more songs, more speeches, more processions.

94
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:27,319
Napoleon had commanded that once a week there should be held something called a

95
00:07:27,319 --> 00:07:32,719
spontaneous demonstration, the object of which was to celebrate the

96
00:07:32,719 --> 00:07:37,159
struggles and triumphs of Animal Farm.

97
00:07:37,159 --> 00:07:41,680
At the appointed time, the animals would leave their work and march round the

98
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:45,559
precincts of the farm in military formation.

99
00:07:45,559 --> 00:07:55,120
With the pigs leading, then the horses, then the cows, then the sheep, and then the poultry.

100
00:07:55,120 --> 00:08:02,559
The dogs flanked the procession, and at the head of all marched Napoleon's black cockerel.

101
00:08:02,559 --> 00:08:08,879
Boxer and Clover always carried between them a green banner marked with the hoof

102
00:08:08,879 --> 00:08:14,600
and the horn and the caption, "Long live Comrade Napoleon."

103
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:19,520
Afterwards, there were recitations of poems composed in Napoleon's honor and a

104
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:24,079
speech by Squealer giving particulars of the latest increases in the production

105
00:08:24,079 --> 00:08:29,840
of foodstuffs, and on occasion a shot was fired from the gun.

106
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:34,120
The sheep were the greatest devotees of the spontaneous demonstration,

107
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:40,239
and if anyone complained, as a few animals sometimes did when no pigs or dogs were near,

108
00:08:40,239 --> 00:08:45,440
that they wasted time and meant a lot of standing about in the cold, the sheep

109
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:53,639
were sure to silence him with a tremendous bleating of "Four legs good, two legs bad."

110
00:08:53,639 --> 00:08:57,959
But by and large, the animals enjoyed these celebrations.

111
00:08:57,959 --> 00:09:04,799
They found it comforting to be reminded that, after all, they were truly their own masters

112
00:09:04,799 --> 00:09:09,000
and that the work they did was for their own benefit.

113
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,239
So that what with the songs, the processions, Squealer's lists of

114
00:09:13,239 --> 00:09:19,319
figures, the thunder of the gun, the crowing of the cockerel, and the fluttering of the flag,

115
00:09:19,319 --> 00:09:26,680
they were able to forget that their bellies were empty, at least part of the time.

116
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:35,680
In April, Animal Farm was proclaimed a republic, and it became necessary to elect a president.

117
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:41,680
There was only one candidate, Napoleon, who was elected unanimously.

118
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:46,079
On the same day, it was given out that fresh documents had been discovered

119
00:09:46,079 --> 00:09:51,040
which revealed further details about Snowball's complicity with Jones.

120
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:56,440
It now appeared that Snowball had not, as the animals had previously imagined,

121
00:09:56,440 --> 00:10:01,600
merely attempted to lose the Battle of the Cowshed by means of a stratagem,

122
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:05,520
but had been openly fighting on Jones's side.

123
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:11,280
In fact, it was he who had actually been the leader of the human forces and had

124
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:16,799
charged into battle with the words, "Long live humanity."

125
00:10:16,799 --> 00:10:18,840
on his lips.

126
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:22,920
The wounds on Snowball's back, which a few of the animals still remembered to

127
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:27,600
have seen, had been inflicted by Napoleon's teeth.

128
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:32,360
In the middle of the summer, Moses the Raven suddenly reappeared on the farm

129
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:35,760
after an absence of several years.

130
00:10:35,760 --> 00:10:41,440
He was quite unchanged, still did no work, and talked in the same strain as

131
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:44,760
ever about Sugar Candy Mountain.

132
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:50,639
He would perch on a stump, flap his black wings, and talk by the hour to

133
00:10:50,639 --> 00:10:52,680
anyone who would listen.

134
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:54,639
"Up there, comrades."

135
00:10:54,639 --> 00:10:59,719
he would say solemnly, pointing to the sky with his large beak.

136
00:10:59,719 --> 00:11:03,959
"Up there, just on the other side of that dark cloud that you can see, there

137
00:11:03,959 --> 00:11:09,600
it lies, Sugar Candy Mountain, that happy country where we poor animals

138
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:12,440
shall rest forever from our labors."

139
00:11:12,440 --> 00:11:16,799
He even claimed to have been there on one of his higher flights and to have

140
00:11:16,799 --> 00:11:24,479
seen the everlasting fields of clover and the linseed cake and lump sugar growing on the hedges.

141
00:11:24,479 --> 00:11:26,959
Many of the animals believed him.

142
00:11:26,959 --> 00:11:31,840
Their lives now, they reasoned, were hungry and laborious.

143
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:38,079
Was it not right and just that a better world should exist somewhere else?

144
00:11:38,079 --> 00:11:42,000
A thing that was difficult to determine was the attitude of the pigs towards
